## <div style="background-color:#1a5276; color:white; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; text-align:center;">
    <h1 style="margin:0; font-size:2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin:5px 0 0 0; font-size:1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Take a Trip to the Panama Canal</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate both fiction and non-fiction stories. Interactive: Conduct interviews on personal interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a story about an Indigenous festival in Panama's comarcas, including sensory details (e.g., "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we saw woven chaquiras, heard traditional flute music, and watched the dancers perform their ancestral steps." "During the Guna Yala celebration, we admired the vibrant molas with intricate patterns and listened to chants while the community shared stories of their ancestors." "At the Emberá cultural festival, we saw beautifully painted jaguá designs on the dancers' skin, heard the rhythmic beat of the drums, and tasted freshly cooked potatoes.").<br>Can conduct a brief interview about a peer's cultural background (e.g., "What traditions does your family celebrate?").<br>Can describe the history of an indigenous group in class discussions (e.g., "I'm interested in learning about the Guna people's history").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color:#1a5276; text-align:center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color:#e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color:#1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color:#1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color:#1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates prior knowledge, introduces the topic, and prepares students for the lesson's speaking focus. The teacher models the target language and checks understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a map of Panama highlighting the Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, and a general area representing indigenous comarcas. The teacher asks students:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What important historical sites do you see on this map?" (Elicit: Panama Canal, Casco Viejo)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Have you visited any of these places? What did you explore?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you remember about the Guna, Ngäbe-Buglé, or Emberá people from our previous lessons?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing a historical site using present simple and simple past. For example: "The Panama Canal is a very famous landmark. It attracts many visitors every year. Last year, I visited the Miraflores Locks, and I learned about its history. It was a really informative tour." The teacher also models how to express future plans using "going to." "Next month, I am going to visit Casco Viejo. I want to explore its ancient architecture." The teacher emphasizes stress in "cultural" and "historical" and minimal pairs like "sit" /ɪ/ and "seat" /iː/ when students speak.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the Panama Canal a recent construction or an ancient monument?" (Expected answer: An ancient monument / Historical.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I am going to explore Casco Viejo next week,' am I talking about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage introduces new language content within an authentic context, linked to the scenario and theme. Students engage with input to grasp meaning and context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue (audio or written on screen) between two Panamanian friends, Maria and Carlos, discussing their travel plans and a recent visit to a cultural site. The dialogue will include target vocabulary (e.g., <i>Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, explore, visit, attract, heritage, destination, cultural, historical, informative</i>) and grammar (present simple, simple past, "going to," possessive 's).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Maria: "Hey Carlos, what are your plans for the upcoming holiday?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Carlos: "Hi Maria! I'm going to visit the Panama Canal next week. My cousin is a visitor from abroad, and he wants to explore this famous landmark. He's also interested in Panama's indigenous heritage."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Maria: "That sounds great! Last month, I went to an Emberá cultural festival. It was so vibrant! I saw beautifully painted jaguá designs on the dancers' skin, and I heard the rhythmic beat of the drums. The community shared stories of their ancestors, which was very informative. Panama's culture is truly rich."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Carlos: "Wow, that's amazing! I'm interested in learning about the Guna people's history too. Maybe we can compare Casco Viejo and Panama Viejo sometime. Casco Viejo is more vibrant, while Panama Viejo is quieter and more serene."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and expressing information about travel plans, historical sites, and cultural experiences in Panama, including distinguishing between informative and descriptive tones.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to/read the dialogue and answer:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Maria and Carlos talking about?" (Expected: travel plans and cultural sites.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Carlos is going to visit the Panama Canal alone." (False) "Maria attended a Ngäbe-Buglé festival." (False - Emberá)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the site with what they explored/will explore (e.g., Panama Canal -> famous landmark, Emberá festival -> jaguá designs, drum beat).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled practice of the target language to develop accuracy in speaking. Activities are scaffolded to build confidence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Describing historical sites, sharing travel plans, and briefly narrating cultural experiences using Present simple, Simple past, Future with "going to," and possessive 's. Vocabulary includes: <i>historical sites, ruins, Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, landmarks, architecture, heritage, monument, destination, visitor, museum, tour, Causeway, explore, visit, attract, learn, describe, cultural, informative, renowned</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Sentence Builder" (5 min)</b><br>Teacher provides sentence stems and students complete them orally using target vocabulary and grammar. For example:<br>a) "Casco Viejo is a __________ because it has beautiful __________." (historical site, architecture)<br>b) "Last year, I __________ the Panama Canal, and I __________ a lot about its __________." (visited, learned, heritage)<br>c) "Next month, my family __________ to __________ the Causeway." (is going, explore)<br>d) "The Guna people's __________ are very vibrant." (molas)<br>Students say their sentences aloud, focusing on correct word stress for words like "cultural" and "historical."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Chain Story" (7 min)</b><br>Students work in pairs. One student starts a sentence about a historical site or a future travel plan using target grammar. The partner repeats the sentence and adds another, using a different grammatical structure or vocabulary word. Encourage use of possessive 's and comparisons (e.g., "Casco Viejo is more vibrant, while Panama Viejo is quieter").<br>Example:<br>Student A: "The Panama Canal attracts many visitors."<br>Student B: "The Panama Canal attracts many visitors. I am going to explore its museum next month."<br>Student A: "I am going to explore its museum next month. My cousin's favorite part was the transit of the ships."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Ask and Describe" (8 min)</b><br>Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a card with a specific historical site or indigenous cultural event (e.g., "Panama Canal," "Casco Viejo," "Ngäbe-Buglé festival," "Guna Yala celebration"). Students take turns asking and answering questions about their card, focusing on description, past experiences, and future plans. They should practice distinguishing /ɪ/ and /iː/ in words like 'visit' and 'see'.<br>Example questions:<br>Student A: "What can you tell me about the Panama Canal?"<br>Student B: "The Panama Canal is a renowned landmark. Many visitors explore it daily. Last year, I learned a lot about its history on a tour."<br>Student A: "Are you going to visit an indigenous festival soon?"<br>Student B: "Yes, I am going to visit the Emberá cultural festival. I want to see their traditional dances and hear their music." (Encourage students to include sensory details from the learning outcome if possible.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language acquired in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency, interaction, and completing the task. This stage directly addresses the lesson's speaking objectives.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-Play: "Panama Travel Interview"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in pairs. One student acts as a "Tourist" planning a trip to Panama, and the other is a "Local Guide" providing information and recommendations. The "Tourist" will ask about historical sites, cultural experiences, and indigenous groups. The "Local Guide" will describe places, share past experiences, and make recommendations, including a brief narration about an indigenous festival or cultural background if prompted.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Tourist Role:</b> Ask about destinations, what to explore, history, and cultural aspects. Use questions like, "What historical sites are you recommending?" "What is Casco Viejo's history like?" "What traditions does your family celebrate?" "I am going to visit the Panama Canal, what should I see?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Local Guide Role:</b> Describe at least two historical sites (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo), use past experiences to recommend places, mention future plans, and narrate a brief story or describe an indigenous festival if the tourist expresses interest in cultural background. Use phrases like, "The Panama Canal is a famous landmark; it was opened in 1914," "Casco Viejo is very attractive to tourists because it was once a vibrant colonial village," "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we saw woven chaquiras, heard traditional flute music, and watched the dancers perform their ancestral steps." "I'm interested in learning about the Guna people's history."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use the target vocabulary (<i>historical sites, Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, explore, visit, attract, heritage, monument, destination, cultural, informative</i>), grammar (present simple, simple past, "going to," possessive 's), and pragmatic functions (understanding intent of questions, comparing sites). The teacher circulates, offering support and noting fluency, accuracy, and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on formative assessment to check learning and provides an opportunity for students to reflect on their performance and learning process.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Panama Travel Interview" role-play for another pair or the teacher. The teacher uses an observation checklist to note individual student participation, use of target vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation. Peers use a simple checklist to evaluate their partner's ability to communicate clearly and complete the task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer & Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (e.g., clear stress in "historical," distinction between /ɪ/ and /iː/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>Panama Canal, historical sites, explore, visitor, cultural</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Present Simple and Simple Past for descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Future with "going to" for plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to conduct a brief interview and respond to questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to describe a historical site or briefly narrate a cultural experience.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active participation in the role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two reflection prompts before leaving class:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you used confidently today when talking about Panama's historical sites or culture?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of speaking about travel plans or historical facts was challenging for you, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process, identify areas for improvement, and connect to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing with your partner help you describe places and share travel plans more fluently today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one question you still have about describing historical sites or cultural events in Panama?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote speaking and interaction? (e.g., the role-play was engaging, students used target vocabulary naturally, pronunciation practice helped).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next speaking tasks? (e.g., some students struggled with narrating sensory details for indigenous festivals, more sentence frames might be useful for complex descriptions, review comparative adjectives for comparing sites).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to orally describe places and cultural experiences. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write descriptive paragraphs about historical sites or cultural festivals in Panama, further solidifying their use of vocabulary and grammar in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>